WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:03.120
hey guys I'm professor Dave and I want
to tell you about chemistry

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.639
what is chemistry why should we
know about it

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:15.330
chemistry is the study of what matter is
made of and how it changes

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:18.330
it's important to know because it describes
everything around us

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:22.160
every material, almost every process. if
we truly want to understand how our

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:22.980
bodies work

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.750
how diseases operate, how to cure them
which foods are nutritious and which are not

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:31.170
we must learn chemistry. unless you
fundamentally understand molecules

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.370
all you can do is repeat things you've
heard. but how do you know for you heard

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:34.880
is right?

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.170
if you know chemistry you can compare
what someone says to how you know that

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:40.340
molecules behave

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.120
if there is a glaring contradiction you
can easily reject false information

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.730
and you won't be taken advantage of for
someone else's personal gain. we should

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.660
be able to think critically like this in
every avenue of life

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.820
be it health, politics or otherwise. with
this general chemistry series

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.039
I have two goals. one is to help high
school and undergraduate students taking

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:00.469
their first chemistry course.

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:04.049
please use these tutorials to supplement
your lectures in any way that is helpful

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:04.629
to you

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:08.500
but because this is introductory
material and given that chemistry is the

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.670
central science

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:14.229
this general chemistry series also meant
for anyone who wants to learn science

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:18.659
with no prior knowledge and for any
reason. I will break down concepts as

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:19.780
simply as possible

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.229
so that anyone can learn them. there will be
moments where we go over practice

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:23.810
problems

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.880
that are more geared towards students in
a class, but feel free to learn these too.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.259
because the predictive power of
mathematics is what proves the

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:32.630
legitimacy of our science

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:35.990
if you're watching this clip and intend
to view the entire series

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.700
please click on the general chemistry
playlist under the Professor dave home page

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.409
to view in the suggested order. when we
check our comprehension after lesson

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:47.880
feel free to pause the clip so you have
plenty of time to think

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.740
before I tell you the answer. if you need
to watch a clip more than once to fully

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:52.579
understand it

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:56.020
that's okay. and if you have any
questions about what you've learned

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.320
feel free to email me

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.799
enjoy your newfound knowledge

